 model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 2: you have used 99 actions; a strong score needs about 54 or fewer. You are 1.8x over the target.\nIf you are not steadily making progress toward the level goal, commit to your single best hypothesis and the shortest sequence that tests it \u2014 do not exhaustively scan rows/columns or enumerate every option. Test one idea, read the result, then decide.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "I need to stop wasting actions on this level. I've used 99 actions already, and the target is about 54. Let me reconsider the approach.\nCurrent state:\n- W (white) at top right (90 pixels) - was green, now white\n- N (green) at middle left... wait, no. Let me re-read the output.\nActually, looking at the output:\n\"Shapes after click:\n  W: pixels=32  (HUD bar)\n  N: pixels=90  (green at top right)\n  W: pixels=162 (white at middle left)\n  p: pixels=90  (purple at bottom right)\n  b: pixels=45  (blue at bottom right corner)\"\nSo after clicking on red block near Y:\n- Y (yellow) -> W (white)\n- W (white) -> N (green)\nCurrent state:\n- N (green) at top right (90 pixels)\n- W (white) at middle left (162 pixels)\n- p (purple) at bottom right (90 pixels)\n- b (blue) at bottom right corner (45 pixels)\nI've been clicking on red blocks to change colors, but the level hasn't completed. Maybe the goal is not to make all shapes white. Let me reconsider.\nLooking at the image, I see:\n- Top right: green rectangle (N)\n- Middle left: white shape (W)\n- Bottom right: purple shape (p)\n- Bottom right corner: blue shape (b)\nMaybe the goal is to move the white shape to overlap with one of the other shapes, like in level 1. In level 1, the solution was to move the white shape to overlap with the green rectangle and rotate it.\nLet me try moving the white shape (currently at middle left) to overlap with the green rectangle (at top right). The white shape is 162 pixels, and the green rectangle is 90 pixels. They might not be the same size, so maybe the goal is different.\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Maybe the goal is to make all shapes the same color by clicking on red blocks in a specific order. Or maybe the goal is to make the white shape a specific color and then move it to a specific position.\nLet me try moving the white shape with arrow keys to see if it moves and if the level completes when it overlaps with another shape.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Current state:\n# - N (green) at top right (90 pixels)\n# - W (white) at middle left (162 pixels)\n# - p (purple) at bottom right (90 pixels)\n# - b (blue) at bottom right corner (45 pixels)\n\n# Let me try moving the white shape (middle left) UP to see if it moves toward the green rectangle\n# The white shape is at middle left, and the green rectangle is at top right.\n# Moving UP might move the white shape upward.\n\nresult = action(['UP'])\nprint(f\"UP: board_changed={result.get('board_changed')}\")\n\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nnodes = seg['nodes']\nprint(\"Shapes after UP:\")\nfor n in nodes:\n    if n['color'] in 'WYNbp' and n['pixels'] > 20:\n        print(f\"  {n['color']}: pixels={n['pixels']}, boundary={n['boundary'][:4]}...\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "to